  `newQ`           Pointer to solution at new time step, i.e. to the new solution within a cell. The the routines exposed to the user, data is always organised as a lexicographically ordered array (left to right, bottom to top, front to back) similar to a regular grid, and the unknowns are ordered AoS. The field always comprises both the unknowns and auxiliary values. In a Finite Volume context, the field never holds halo data.   `double*`
  `oldQ`           Pointer to solution at the old (current) time step. In a Finite Volume context, the field never holds halo data.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               `double*`
  `deltaQ`         Pointer to difference between `newQ` and `oldQ`, i.e. $Q^{(new)}-Q^{(old)}$. As  never evolves auxiliary variables, this field does only store the unknowns of the PDE as AoS.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 `double*`
  `dQdt`           Pointer to derivative of the unknown. As  never evolves auxiliary variables, this field does only store the unknowns of the PDE as AoS. I'm aware that the name is inconsistent with the naming below, but `dQdtimeStepSize` would be weird.                                                                                                                                                                                   `double*`
  `oldQWithHalo`   Exists only in a Finite Volume context. Different to `oldQ`, this field holds halo data.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       `double*`
  `Q`              See discussion around `newQ` and `oldQ`. I use a plain `Q` if there is no old or new data, or if it is implicitly clear that we evaluate the solution at a certain (old) point and plan to step into the future.                                                                                                                                                                                                               `double*`
  `timeStamp`      I decided to stick to this (long) identifier rather than a plain `t` as you find it in math papers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            `double`
  `timeStepSize`   I decided to stick to this (long) identifier rather than a plain `dt` ($\Delta t$) as you find it in math papers.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              `double`

