To determine if the solver is (close to) stationary, we evaluate the
difference between the gradient of $\phi$ after each Poisson step. For
this, we tweak the coupling term:

The new computation preserves the semantics of the old one plus computes
a value `delta` which it then pipes into the Poisson solver object.
Here, we once again exploit our knowledge about 's internal working and
naming conventions. To make the whole thing finally work, we tweak the
Poisson solver:

-   We let the Poisson solver keep track of the gradient deltas.
    Actually, we use two deltas. `_currentMaxGradientDelta` is used to
    analyse the current delta which we change throughout the grid
    traversal, while `_previousMaxGradientDelta` holds the result from
    the previous grid traversal.

-   We introduce a new function `reportGradientDelta` that accepts the
    `delta` as we have computed it above.

-   We hook into `startTimeStep` to roll the deltas over.

-   We overwrite `getAdmissibleTimeStepSize`. If the two delta values do
    not differ that much anymore, we assume we have converged and we use
    the standard time step size the solver would use. If the two delta
    values differ significantly, we assume that we have not yet convered
    and thus use a tiny time step size.

Case study: Coupling finite volumes with Runge-Kutta DG (limiting) {#section:exahype:fv:limiting}
------------------------------------------------------------------

Even simple initial conditions can make a Discontinuous Galerking solver
fail: The higher order solution representation is not capable to resolve
shocks properly (or steep gradients), and the solution therefore starts
to oscillation. At the same time, experiments with Finite Volumes yield
solutions which a subject to very strong numerical diffusion. The
solution quality is not good.

There are two fundamental approaches to tackle this dilemma: On the one
hand, we can introduce artificial damping terms, i.e. numerical
diffusion, or truncation terms for the Riemann solvers such that these
oscillations are eliminated. On the other hand, we can accept that both
DG and FV have their pros and cons and try to use each solver where it
unfolds its full potential. This is the prime strategy in the original
 project.

To realise this a posteriori limiting (which really will become an a
priori limiting), we follow the same pattern that we exploited in the
previous case study: We define two different solvers, and couple them
with each other. Different to the previous setup, we however make all of
our operations local:

-   The Finite Volume solver is our fallback strategy and we therefore
    neither solve with Finite Volumes everywhere in the computational
    domain nor should do we store the corresponding data.

-   The FV and the DG solver are not coupled everywhere but only in
    boundary regions, i.e. in regions where the FV solver and the DG
    solver kind of agree solution-wisely.

We will effectively end up with a domain decomposition where parts of
the computational domain are handled by the DG solver and the other
parts are handled with FV. To realise this, we need a marker, i.e. a
label per cell which tells us, similar to a multi-phase flow, which
solver rules in this particular cell. Then, we construct two solvers
that are coupled with each other similar to the previous case study. The
marker will determine which solver is "valid" in which cell, where they
are active, and where the two solvers are coupled with each other.

For the coupling of two solvers, it is advantageous not to work with
 solver instances directly. Instead, I recommend that you specialise the
solver of your choice such that you can add further features to this
class. In Python, this is straightforward. The line

is replaced with

All remarks below refer to objects instantiating the specialisation of
the FV and DG solver, respectively, rather than a direct specialisation.

##### Two solvers {#two-solvers .unnumbered}

In this simple setup, we just create a DG solver and then add an
additional Finite Volume solver. The latter works with a patch size of
$2p+1$ where $p$ is the spatial DG order. You might want to use a
different patch size, but this choice usually yields time step sizes in
the FV discretisation which is similar to the DG scheme. We add both of
them to the project and refer to them as `dg_solver` and `fv_solver`
objects in the Python script. You can use any solver you like, but I
strongly recommend that you use the same time stepping scheme for both
solvers. All the explanations below refer to adaptive time stepping. You
might have to alter some bits and pieces if you use a different time
stepping scheme.

##### The marker {#the-marker .unnumbered}

The marker is determined by a quantity $\alpha$ which solves the
equation $$- \Delta \alpha = C < 0 
 \qquad
 \text{s.t.} \ 
 0 \leq \alpha  \leq 1, \ 
 \alpha  | _{\partial \Omega} = 0$$ via a cell-centered finite
differences scheme. In the cells where we definitely need the Finite
Volume scheme, we set $\alpha
 = 1$. The higher $\alpha$, the more sure we are that the FV scheme
yields the correct result.

 is shipped with a pre-manufactured solver for this Poisson setup which
takes care of all the details---including the mapping of the continuous
$\alpha$ value onto the states which solver is active where---and also
has more documentation):

##### Control the marker {#control-the-marker .unnumbered}

The marker by default will be $\alpha =0$ in the present configuration.
As a first step, we hence have to reset it to $\alpha =1$ where we want
the FV scheme to dominate, i.e. where we definitely know that it is the
FV scheme that we need. Technically, we realise this by a postprocessing
step on the respective solver:

##### Translate marker outcome onto grid {#translate-marker-outcome-onto-grid .unnumbered}

The marker helps us to identify where which solver is active. This is
information we next want to project onto the grid, i.e. we want to find
out which mesh cells have to store which data. For this, we have to
augment the marker object within the cell which is an object that is
always associated with any  mesh cell and has nothing to do with the
stuff that we call "a marker" above.

It seems to be (and is) quite complicated that we move the storage
information from the marker into the grid. However, there are two good
reasons for this:

-   On the one hand, we might want to tailor the information delivered
    by the marker towards our needs. It is not even prescribed how many
    solvers in total are guided by one marker!

-   On the other hand, we need the mesh storage information later within
    the solver, where we have to access to the newly generated Poisson
    equation. We have access to the mesh marker however.

Ganz ganz wichtig ist, dass der Poisson solver als erstes kommt vor dem
DG und dem FV Zeugs!

##### Restrict the solvers {#restrict-the-solvers .unnumbered}

With the marker at hand, we can instruct the solver not to solve their
equation in certain regions. We can also ensure that they don't store
data where it actually is not required. The cell marker as introduced by
`ConstrainedPoissonEquationForMarkerOnCells` provides all the necessary
information to guide the data flow.

We don't want to alter the core logic of the data flow, but actually
veto for some cells that data is stored or loaded. There are various
ways to achieve this. I propose to plug into
`_load_cell_data_default_guard()` and
`_store_cell_data_default_guard()`.

